  "statement": "Prometheus correctly identifies that top-down bureaucratic mandates foster regulatory capture, yet the proposed litigation-based model fails to account for the physical reality of irreversible, non-linear system failure. Symmetric litigation exposure operates on the premise that a firm remains a viable entity capable of paying damages after a catastrophe. If an autonomous system triggers an existential event, the firm and its assets effectively cease to exist as functional economic agents, rendering post-mortem liability a mathematical nullity. Relying on litigation to prevent a terminal breach is like attempting to regulate a nuclear meltdown by suing the power plant operator after the core has already vaporized.\n\nMy proposed open-source auditing cooperative bypasses these gatekeeping risks by functioning as a decentralized, public-domain utility rather than a regulatory agency. Instead of granting bureaucrats the power to issue or deny permits, this cooperative provides standardized, empirical safety suites that any developer can access to verify their model’s internal logic before reaching the public. By lowering the cost of rigorous testing for small innovators, we create a market-agnostic safety floor that makes compliance a technical commodity rather than a high-barrier-to-entry legal hurdle. This approach ensures that safety is achieved through verifiable, pre-deployment mechanistic interpretability, not through the hope that a bankrupt firm can compensate humanity for a permanent loss of agency.\n\nLitigation functions as a lagging indicator, whereas pre-deployment verification provides a leading indicator of risk. Because advanced systems can exhibit emergent behaviors that are not visible in simple output-based auditing, we must mandate that developers demonstrate stability within their internal neural representations before reaching a 10% market penetration threshold. This framework forces developers to internalize safety costs at the R&D stage, where they can be effectively mitigated, rather than externalizing them onto a public that has no mechanism for redress once an irreversible failure occurs. By moving the site of intervention to the pre-deployment phase, we transform safety from a performative legal claim into an engineering requirement.",
